Right now, the strategies one pursues are not greatly different for any of 
the classes.  If I like to go down quickly with my wizard, that's OK, or 
slowly with a barbarian.  If I want to play a tourist, and find an altar on
the first level, I can sacrifice at it all I want.  There are infinite 
strategies possible, and I can run any of them.

Suppose now that we say that all wizard's can't use weapons, or at least 
have significant penalties.
As of now, the variables when choosing a starting character are intrinsics, 
starting inventory, and characteristics.  If one wants to play a character
with a large initial selection of magic, for example, one is constrained to 
play a wizard and start without the better weapon and cold resistance of a 
valykrie, or the intrinsic blessing knowledge and chain mail of the priest.
The selection of characters that we have now provides a reasonable set of 
choices among these.

The proposed change, however, is that we add rather significant limitations 
and advantages, so that rather than consisting of relatively minor changes,
the entire nature of the game will shift depending on your class.  I object 
to this because there are a limited number of classes; if we increase the 
variety between playing them, then this restricts the strategies available 
to one per character. 

There is also the matter of conception.  Why can't my mage have learned to 
swing a sword?  Why can't my barbarian have been literate?  If that's what 
I want to play?  Balance?  Right now, the balance is fine and I can play 
a heavy-magic mage or a wizard who knows his way around with a sword.   
